<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>reliable broadcast on Eventually Read</title><link>https://articles-review.github.io/tags/reliable-broadcast/</link><description>Recent content in reliable broadcast on Eventually Read</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://articles-review.github.io/tags/reliable-broadcast/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Two-step optimism, three-step totality: paired witnesses for reliable broadcast</title><link>https://articles-review.github.io/posts/optimistic-reliable-broadcast-three-steps/</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://articles-review.github.io/posts/optimistic-reliable-broadcast-three-steps/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Reliable broadcast lets one designated party, the broadcaster, hand a value to &lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mi&gt;n&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;annotation encoding="application/x-tex"&gt;n&lt;/annotation&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-base"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-strut" style="height:0.4306em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; parties so that the honest ones end up agreeing on it, even when up to &lt;span class="katex"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-mathml"&gt;&lt;math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"&gt;&lt;semantics&gt;&lt;mrow&gt;&lt;mi&gt;f&lt;/mi&gt;&lt;/mrow&gt;&lt;annotation encoding="application/x-tex"&gt;f&lt;/annotation&gt;&lt;/semantics&gt;&lt;/math&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-base"&gt;&lt;span class="katex-strut" style="height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.1076em;"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; of them are Byzantine, meaning they can behave arbitrarily. DAG-based consensus protocols run one broadcast instance per block per round. Every step a broadcast takes therefore shows up in commit latency, and every step by which one honest party can lag behind another shows up in how long timeouts must be.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>